-[F4] [[cor-determinant-is-a-polynomial-in-the-matrix-entries]]: for every finite matrix size the determinant is given by its Leibniz permutation polynomial; in particular, for a $3\times3$ matrix $M=(m_{ij})$, $\det M=\sum_{\sigma\in S_3}\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)\prod_{i=1}^3m_{\sigma(i),i}$, where $S_3$ is the set of permutations of $\{1,2,3\}$ and $\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)$ is $1$ or $-1$ according as $\sigma$ is even or odd.
+[F4] [[cor-determinant-is-a-polynomial-in-the-matrix-entries]]: for each fixed $n\ge1$, the determinant is the polynomial $\sum_{\tau\in S_n}\operatorname{sgn}(\tau)\prod_{i<n}b_{\tau(i),i}$, with all $n$ rows and columns indexed by $0,\ldots,n-1$. To use spatial indices $1,2,3$, set $b_{ri}=m_{r+1,i+1}$ for $r,i<3$ and associate to each permutation $\sigma$ of $\{1,2,3\}$ the permutation $\tau(i)=\sigma(i+1)-1$ of $\{0,1,2\}$. This bijection preserves inversions and therefore signs, and reindexes the three factors. Thus $\det M=\sum_{\sigma\in\operatorname{Sym}(\{1,2,3\})}\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)\prod_{j=1}^3m_{\sigma(j),j}$. The supplier product $i<n$ includes exactly $n$ factors.
 
